WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.220
Suppose an infection leaves you a thousand times more cells that fit a virus.

2
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.100
Is your second response a thousand times faster?

3
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.540
The first film ended with an antigen choosing the few cells that fit.

4
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.960
But a few cells cannot fight an infection.

5
00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:23.680
How do a few become enough?

6
00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:26.930
A chosen cell divides into two.

7
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:29.630
Each of those divides again.

8
00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:33.170
With every round of division, the clone doubles.

9
00:00:34.883 --> 00:00:39.343
So the number of cells is the starting number, doubled once for every round.

10
00:00:40.763 --> 00:00:42.823
Enough rounds turn a few cells into millions.

11
00:00:44.017 --> 00:00:45.517
Now draw it as a graph.

12
00:00:47.037 --> 00:00:48.137
Days run along the bottom.

13
00:00:49.637 --> 00:00:50.957
Cell number runs up the side.

14
00:00:52.237 --> 00:00:54.317
Each gridline is ten times the one below.

15
00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.850
On this axis, steady doubling is a straight climb.

16
00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:04.970
A first response starts from a few cells, so it needs many doublings.

17
00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:07.390
It takes days to build.

18
00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:12.810
When the infection is cleared, most of the expanded cells die.

19
00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.950
But memory B cells and memory T cells remain.

20
00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:20.810
There are many more of them than the clone started with.

21
00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:26.213
Suppose the memory pool is a thousand times bigger than the starting clone.

22
00:01:27.633 --> 00:01:30.533
How many fewer doublings does a second response need?

23
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.900
About ten fewer. A thousandfold head start covers only about ten doublings.

24
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:47.480
So it saves those rounds, not a thousandfold of the time.

25
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.010
So the second curve starts high and reaches the line sooner.

26
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:57.050
This second, secondary response needs fewer rounds of division.

27
00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:03.470
Real secondary responses are usually larger too, for reasons this simple model

28
00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:04.470
leaves out.

29
00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:07.983
This doubling picture is a simplified model.

30
00:02:09.183 --> 00:02:11.223
It leaves out cell death during the climb.

31
00:02:12.503 --> 00:02:17.463
And many memory cells, though not all kinds, respond faster than first-time cells.

32
00:02:18.617 --> 00:02:20.357
Where does a head start come from?

33
00:02:21.897 --> 00:02:26.837
Compare two mice. One gets a harmless viral protein, as a vaccine.

34
00:02:28.237 --> 00:02:31.857
Its own fitting cells expand, and memory cells stay behind.

35
00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:37.690
The other mouse gets antibody taken from an immunized mouse.

36
00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:40.370
It is protected at once.

37
00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:45.030
But the antibody is broken down, and no memory cells were made.

38
00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:49.390
Protection your own lymphocytes make is active immunity.

39
00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:54.310
Protection by antibody from another body is passive immunity.

40
00:02:55.483 --> 00:02:57.863
Six months later, both meet the virus.

41
00:02:59.123 --> 00:03:01.603
The vaccinated mouse starts high on the curve.

42
00:03:02.743 --> 00:03:06.143
The other starts from the bottom, like a mouse that never met it.
